#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069998 :tutorial/content "To simplify radical expressions, we can use the quotient rule for radicals, which states \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\sqrt {a}} {\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac a b}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}$\n\nFirst, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}  = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{16w^2}} {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{25}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression under the radical as a perfect square and taking its square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{16{w}^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(4w)^{2}}}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{|4|\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot |w|}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|w|}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7bfc07d9-feb0-5bcc-b3f0-50bb42eb2b6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6691 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069999 :tutorial/content "The quotient rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{ \\dfrac a b } = \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[3]{a} }{ \\sqrt[3]{b} }\n$$\n\nIn general, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{ \\dfrac a b } = \\dfrac{ \\sqrt[n]{a} }{ \\sqrt[n]{b} }\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a1d735da-05d5-5959-9226-229b7c29c497" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6692 :tutorial/title "The Quotient Rule for Cube Roots and Other Radicals"}]}
